ion of the city itself  right  we sort of erase or marginalize the  people who've been in these  neighborhoods for many years you know  mostly um  latinx or black we make it safe we bring  these amenities these restaurants these  bars we sort of idealize this past this  sort of industrial past and our culture  but really it's about bringing in this  kind of investment right the kind of  tourism dollars consumer dollars  rental dollars that didn't exist there  before  as a reaction to the bland staleness of  the suburbs right like you know all the  things that people are saying in the  1980s particularly in hardcore punk of  you know this is just boring and  horrible and terrible it's about tv and  fast food  uh whereas this authentic urban  experience exists that i can access  big picture just to kind of uh wrap the  conversation  in thinking about the next you know 10  20 years  um what what do you see as sort of the  shifts  in terms of rural suburban and urban  that seem to be guided by the interests  of the people that are now becoming  homeowners will the shift to  urbanization continue or  do you expect it to change or reverse  well one is there are fewer homeowners  right so  probably i suspect you're around my age  or younger our generation are not going  to be the generation of homeowners in  the same way because we're not being  facilitated by the market or the  government to own homes um so we may  inherit homes many of us reckons where  you know some largely white people will  get our parents our grandparents homes  but home ownership i think as an idea  might have to be really reconfigured  because people are desiring some some um  intermediary between urban and suburban  experiences convenience  locality but also some sort of idea of  authenticity  but the pandemic also really throws us  into high relief right we've seen  i i think the exodus is maybe uh  overemphasized but people have moved out  of the city  seeing it as sort of the home of  pestilence right you know this kind of  almost  19th century idea of the city so it's  hard to say and anything about public  health or other things what that might  really mean  we may really see people kind of move  far out and go quote-unquote back to the  land  like a lot of people did in the 1970s  yeah that's uh that's a question which  i think as you point out we just don't  don't really know yet it's a new it's a  new question uh we've been speaking with  kyle rhys mandel who's a senior  university lecturer in history  at the new jersey institute of  technology the book is neighborhood of  fear the suburban crisis in american  culture  1975-2001 kyle so great having you on i  really appreciate it  thanks for me i really enjoyed it
 

